# Who gets asked to review what.
#
# Same rules as two-bot and two-web: handles are GitHub *teams* in the
# two-gaming org, never individuals, so a review request never dies because one
# account is asleep. Teams are created by scripts/setup-github.sh in two-bot;
# see docs/GITHUB.md there for the list and who is in each.
#
# A rule naming a team that does not exist, or a team without write access to
# this repo, is silently dropped by GitHub. Nothing warns you. `setup-github.sh
# --verify` reads this file and fails on either case.

# Default: the design system belongs to the Designer.
*                           @two-gaming/design

# Tokens are the contract. `resources/css/two.css` in two-web is a copy of
# tokens/two.css, so a change here lands in the live site's colours, spacing and
# type scale. Frontend sees it before it does.
/tokens/                    @two-gaming/design @two-gaming/frontend

# The gates that make the accessibility claim mean something. QA wrote them and
# QA reviews changes to them - "make CI green" is the easiest thing in the repo
# to get wrong on purpose.
/.github/workflows/         @two-gaming/design @two-gaming/qa
/tools/                     @two-gaming/design @two-gaming/qa

# Source-control posture. Changing who reviews what, or removing a hook, is not
# a design decision.
/.github/CODEOWNERS         @two-gaming/founding-engineer
/.githooks/                 @two-gaming/founding-engineer
/.gitleaks.toml             @two-gaming/founding-engineer
